     "type": "intro",
     "title": "Seneca's Unsettling Advice: Rehearse the Bad Thing Now",
     "content": "The Stoic Seneca, writing to console and prepare a friend, gives advice that sounds, at first hearing, like terrible mental health guidance: let nothing be unexpected by us, he says; our minds should range ahead into the future and consider every setback that could possibly happen, before it happens. Marcus Aurelius opens a book of his own private notebook the same way, listing in advance the difficult people he expects to meet that day. Epictetus's own version, briefer, appears in his instructions about the public bathhouse: picture the splashers, the shovers, and the thieves before you ever walk through the door.\n\nA prior lesson in this book mentioned this practice briefly, as one step in a morning routine. This lesson slows down and does the exercise properly, because the surface-level description above should worry you. Deliberately imagining a friend's death, a job loss, an exile, sounds exactly like the thing therapists spend their careers trying to talk anxious people out of doing. How can rehearsing catastrophe on purpose be a cornerstone Stoic practice for equanimity, rather than a recipe for the very dread it claims to prevent?\n\nThe answer, this lesson argues, has almost nothing to do with the content of what is imagined and almost everything to do with the structure of the exercise: a specific, bounded, four-part procedure with a defined beginning and a defined end. That structure is precisely what separates premeditatio malorum from the anxious rehearsal it superficially resembles - and precisely what collapses when people try the practice without it.",
