     "type": "example",
     "title": "Two Colleagues, the Same Bad News",
     "content": "Picture two colleagues who each learn, on the same afternoon, that a major client account might be cancelled next quarter.\n\nColleague A has never rehearsed this possibility. The news triggers an immediate, unbounded spiral: I will be fired, I will never find another job this good, my family will suffer for years. Notice the shape - it moves instantly from one specific event to global, permanent conclusions, it stays in phase one indefinitely, and it pairs with paralysis rather than any decisive next action. This is functionally indistinguishable from clinical rumination.\n\nColleague B, months earlier, ran a brief and deliberate premeditation on exactly this scenario: the specific loss named, sorted (my preparation and my professional relationships are up to me; the client's internal budget decision is not), a response rehearsed (I would tighten our departmental budget and pursue two new leads I have already identified), and released. When the news actually arrives, Colleague B feels real, appropriate disappointment - Stoicism never asks for numbness - but a workable response is already loaded rather than needing to be improvised under stress. The scene, in Epictetus's theater language, has already been blocked.\n\nContemporary psychology offers a partial parallel worth naming carefully: research on so-called defensive pessimism suggests that some anxious individuals genuinely perform better when permitted to rehearse worst-case scenarios in a structured way, rather than being told simply to think positively. That is real support for the Stoic mechanism. But the boundary case matters equally: for people prone to clinical anxiety, unstructured what if rehearsal easily slides into rumination without phases two through four holding it in shape, which is exactly why the discipline, not just the imagery, is the active ingredient.",
