     "type": "application",
     "title": "Practice: Run One Bounded Premeditation Tonight",
     "content": "This exercise runs the four-phase discipline once, deliberately, on paper, with a hard time limit so it cannot drift into open-ended worry.\n\n1. Set a timer for three minutes before you begin. This is not optional - the boundary is part of the exercise, not a suggestion.\n\n2. Anticipate: write down one specific, genuinely plausible difficulty you may face in the next week or month. Not everything, one thing, named precisely.\n\n3. Sort: list, in two short columns, what would be up to you if this happened (your preparation, your next action, your judgment about it) and what would not be up to you (other people's decisions, timing, chance).\n\n4. Rehearse: write two or three sentences describing the response you would actually choose, calmly, without melodrama and without minimizing that it would be genuinely hard.\n\n5. Release: write the reservation formula in your own words - something like, this may happen or it may not - and then close the notebook and immediately do one unrelated, present-moment task: reply to an email, wash a dish, take a short walk. The pivot away from the imagined future is part of the exercise, not an afterthought.\n\nA necessary caution: if you have a diagnosed anxiety disorder in which imagining feared outcomes tends to spiral rather than resolve, this exercise may not behave the way it is described here, and it is worth trying only with a therapist's guidance, or not at all. Knowing which camp you are in before you start is itself a piece of self-knowledge worth having.",
