     "type": "application",
     "title": "Practice: Sort Symptom Relief From Systemic Action",
     "content": "This closing exercise applies the whole book's arc to one real stressor while explicitly guarding against the quietism error in either direction.\n\n1. Choose one genuine current stressor - a workplace conflict, a financial pressure, a strained relationship, an injustice you have witnessed or experienced.\n\n2. Run Track One, the personal track: apply the ABC analysis and the dichotomy of control privately, exactly as earlier lessons taught. Separate the event from your judgment about it; choose a response that manages your own emotional reaction without waiting for anything external to change first. This step is legitimate and useful on its own terms - do not skip it out of concern that it looks too individualistic.\n\n3. Run Track Two, the collective track, separately and explicitly: ask whether any part of this stressor's underlying cause is structurally changeable through organized, collective, up to us together action - a workplace policy, a law, a shared norm, a union, a petition, a vote. If so, name one concrete step you could take on that separate track this month.\n\n4. Keep the two tracks visibly distinct on paper. Track One is not an excuse to skip Track Two. Track Two is not a reason to neglect Track One while waiting for systemic change that may take years.\n\n5. Review both tracks together and notice: managing your own judgment well and pursuing collective change well are not competing projects. Epictetus trained the first with extraordinary care. This capstone's whole argument is that the second deserves the same seriousness, on its own separate track, rather than being quietly absorbed into the first.",
